## Jackson 2.10.2 API for Controlling `@JacksonInject` Override Behavior

Here are the findings for each of your questions:

### 1. `NopAnnotationIntrospector` -- Methods Available to Override

File: `com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.introspect.NopAnnotationIntrospector` in `/root/.m2/repository/com/fasterxml/jackson/core/jackson-databind/2.10.2/jackson-databind-2.10.2.jar`

`NopAnnotationIntrospector` is an **abstract** class that extends `AnnotationIntrospector`. It itself defines almost nothing -- just a constructor and `version()`. However, since it extends `AnnotationIntrospector`, all of `AnnotationIntrospector`'s methods are available to override, including both:

```java
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m);
public Object findInjectableValueId(AnnotatedMember m);
```

The current Druid code (`/root/druid/core/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/guice/GuiceAnnotationIntrospector.java`) only overrides `findInjectableValueId`, which returns a Guice `Key` object as the injectable value ID.

### 2. `findInjectableValue` Returning `JacksonInject.Value` -- YES, it exists

In `AnnotationIntrospector` (the parent of `NopAnnotationIntrospector`), there are **two** methods:

| Method | Return Type | Purpose |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| `findInjectableValueId(AnnotatedMember)` | `Object` | Legacy/simple API -- returns just the ID |
| `findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember)` | `JacksonInject.Value` | Newer API -- returns a rich `Value` object with both the ID and `useInput` control |

The default implementation of `findInjectableValue` in `AnnotationIntrospector` delegates to `findInjectableValueId`:

```java
// Decompiled from bytecode:
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m) {
    Object id = findInjectableValueId(m);
    if (id != null) {
        return JacksonInject.Value.forId(id);  // useInput defaults to null (unset)
    }
    return null;
}
```

**Crucially**, `JacksonInject.Value.forId(id)` calls `construct(id, null)`, which sets `_useInput` to `null`. When `_useInput` is `null`, the `willUseInput(boolean defaultValue)` method returns whatever default is passed -- meaning JSON input CAN override the injected value by default.

The actual deserialization pipeline (`POJOPropertiesCollector`) calls `findInjectableValue` (not `findInjectableValueId`), so this is the method that matters.

### 3. `JacksonInject.Value` and Setting `useInput` to False -- YES

`JacksonInject.Value` in `/root/.m2/repository/com/fasterxml/jackson/core/jackson-annotations/2.10.2/jackson-annotations-2.10.2.jar` has these key methods:

```java
public class JacksonInject.Value {
    // Construction
    static Value empty();
    static Value construct(Object id, Boolean useInput);
    static Value from(JacksonInject annotation);
    static Value forId(Object id);           // useInput = null (unset)

    // Immutable "withers"
    Value withId(Object id);
    Value withUseInput(Boolean useInput);    // KEY METHOD

    // Accessors
    Object getId();
    Boolean getUseInput();
    boolean hasId();
    boolean willUseInput(boolean defaultValue);  // returns _useInput if set, else defaultValue
}
```

**To prevent JSON input from overriding an injected value**, you can construct a `JacksonInject.Value` with `useInput` set to `false`:

```java
// Option A: construct directly
JacksonInject.Value.construct(myGuiceKey, Boolean.FALSE);

// Option B: use withUseInput on an existing value
JacksonInject.Value.forId(myGuiceKey).withUseInput(Boolean.FALSE);
```

When `useInput` is `Boolean.FALSE`, the `willUseInput(defaultValue)` method returns `false` regardless of the default, which tells Jackson: "do NOT allow JSON input to set this property; always use the injected value."

### 4. `hasInjectableValueId` -- NO, does NOT exist

There is **no** method called `hasInjectableValueId` in `AnnotationIntrospector`, `NopAnnotationIntrospector`, or anywhere in the Jackson 2.10.2 API. The methods available are:

- `findInjectableValueId(AnnotatedMember)` -- returns `Object` (null if not injectable)
- `findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember)` -- returns `JacksonInject.Value` (null if not injectable)

However, `JacksonInject.Value` does have a `hasId()` method, which returns `true` if the `_id` field is non-null.

### Practical Recommendation for Druid

The current `GuiceAnnotationIntrospector` at `/root/druid/core/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/guice/GuiceAnnotationIntrospector.java` overrides only `findInjectableValueId`. To control whether JSON can override injected values, you should **switch to overriding `findInjectableValue`** instead, which lets you return a `JacksonInject.Value` with `useInput = false`:

```java
@Override
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m) {
    if (m.getAnnotation(JacksonInject.class) == null) {
        return null;
    }

    // ... existing Guice Key resolution logic ...

    // Return Value with useInput=false to prevent JSON override
    return JacksonInject.Value.construct(guiceKey, Boolean.FALSE);
}
```

Note that `AnnotationIntrospectorPair.findInjectableValue` uses a simple "primary wins, then try secondary" strategy -- if the primary introspector returns non-null, the secondary is never consulted. This means the `GuiceAnnotationIntrospector` should return the complete `JacksonInject.Value` including the `useInput` setting.
